WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.580
Welcome to Planet Terri and Daily Episode 52.

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:04.680
It's April 29th, 2026.

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:06.980
Let's see what the latest research is telling us.

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:09.600
A blood test using artificial intelligence models

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:11.860
is showing early promise for monitoring

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:14.540
a rare childhood bone and soft tissue cancer.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:17.640
Researchers have developed a liquid biopsy test

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:19.320
that uses artificial intelligence

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:22.160
to detect molecular signals of this rare cancer

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:24.260
that mainly affects children and adolescents.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.420
The approach provides a less invasive alternative

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:30.040
to traditional imaging or tissue biopsies,

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:31.720
which can be especially burdensome

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:33.820
for young patients already facing treatment.

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:36.700
According to the report in fierce biotech,

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:38.680
the test could help track disease progression

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:41.300
and spot signs of relapse or treatment response

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:43.200
earlier than current methods allow.

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:45.440
What makes this particularly interesting

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:47.520
is how the machine learning models are trained

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:49.800
to identify subtle patterns and blood samples

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:51.620
that human analysts might overlook.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:54.900
If the technology holds up in larger validation studies,

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:56.000
it could reduce the need

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:57.320
for frequent hospital visits

00:00:57.320 --> 00:00:59.840
and lower the overall physical strain on these kids.

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:02.820
This is still early promise rather than a finished tool,

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:05.040
but it shows how combining diagnostics

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:06.260
with artificial intelligence

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:08.260
might change the monitoring landscape

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:09.680
in pediatric oncology.

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:12.460
While that story highlights how technology

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:14.240
can ease the burden on young patients,

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:17.380
today's news also touches on weight management challenges

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:20.560
that many adults face after stopping certain medications.

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:23.780
A simple, outpatient, endoscopic procedure

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:26.820
already in use for type two diabetes patients in Europe

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:29.920
helped individuals retain most of their weight loss

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:32.820
after discontinuing Eli Lilly's tears appetite.

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:35.940
The study published in fierce biotech positions

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:38.220
this technique as a potential bridge strategy

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:40.280
for people who must stop glucagon

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:42.780
like peptide-1 receptor agonist medications.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:45.500
Rather than seeing rapid rebound,

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:47.500
the participants maintained the majority

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:48.980
of their metabolic improvements

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:51.680
even after the drug was no longer part of their regimen.

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:55.140
The procedure itself is minimally invasive

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:57.220
and performed on an outpatient basis,

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:00.140
which makes it an interesting non-drug intervention

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:03.480
in the broader conversation about sustained metabolic health.

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:07.120
It is worth noting that this builds on an existing technique

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:09.900
rather than introducing an entirely new technology

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:11.660
which could mean faster adoption

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:14.100
if further trials confirm the benefits.

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:16.700
For patients worried about long-term dependence

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:17.640
on these medications,

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:20.460
the finding offers a practical physiological option

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:21.660
worth watching closely,

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:25.340
staying with interventions that aim to produce lasting change,

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:28.180
a major gene editing trial just delivered results

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:30.920
that could reshape how we think about one-time treatments.

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:33.840
Inteliatheraputics reported that it's

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:35.760
in vivo CRISPR-based therapy,

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:37.780
Lawnville-Guron-Z-Climmerin,

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:41.740
met the primary endpoint in a pivotal phase three clinical trial.

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:45.040
The company has now begun a rolling submission

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:46.400
to the Food and Drug Administration

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:50.200
advancing the prospect of one-time gene editing treatments

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:52.120
delivered directly inside the body.

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:56.760
Unlike approaches that require removing cells editing them

00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:58.120
in a lab and returning them,

00:02:58.260 --> 00:02:59.500
this therapy works in vivo

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:02.020
which simplifies the entire process considerably.

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:04.380
The results are being described as compelling

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:07.580
and they represent meaningful progress for CRISPR technologies

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:10.760
that avoid the complexities of X vivo cell manipulation.

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:12.040
If approved,

00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:13.620
this would be an important step

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:15.620
toward making precise genetic corrections

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:17.080
more accessible and scalable

00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:18.520
for certain genetic conditions.

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:21.460
From precise molecular tools inside the body,

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:23.620
we shift to the larger environmental cost

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:25.380
of building those very technologies.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:28.600
Mining for lithium cobalt and other critical minerals

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:31.040
needed for green energy and digital devices

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:33.740
is creating severe, largely untracked,

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:35.400
environmental, and health impacts.

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:37.620
These harms fall disproportionately

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:39.780
on the world's most vulnerable communities

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:40.920
according to a new report

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:43.160
from the United Nations University Institute

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:44.640
for Water Environment and Health.

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:46.920
The report draws clear parallels

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:49.280
to historical patterns of resource extraction

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:51.140
and warns that current practices

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:54.520
risk repeating past injustices on an even larger scale.

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:57.520
Scientists are calling for improved tracking systems,

00:03:57.680 --> 00:03:59.060
better accountability mechanisms,

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:01.720
and greater recognition of how these supply chains

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:03.620
affect public health and affected regions.

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:07.060
The findings remind us that the push for better health technologies

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:08.780
can sometimes create new inequities

00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:11.860
far from the laboratories where those innovations are developed.

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:14.520
The same drive for better health technologies

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:16.920
can sometimes create new inequities closer to home

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:19.520
and new report tracks both progress and fragility

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:20.960
in United States health equity.

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:24.460
A report released this week documents measurable successes

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:27.620
in reducing health disparities across United States states

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:29.760
between 2022 and 2024.

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:32.860
Experts caution, however, that recent policy shifts

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:34.480
could reverse those fragile gains

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:36.480
in a relatively short period of time.

00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:39.140
The improvements were real and quantifiable,

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:42.320
yet they appear vulnerable to changes in funding priorities

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:43.700
in public health infrastructure.

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:46.440
This highlights the importance of sustained attention

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:49.200
beyond short-term wins if we want health equity gains

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:50.420
to become lasting ones.

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:53.680
Policy decisions also shape how conditions like autism

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:55.900
are understood and supported at the federal level.

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:58.820
A restructured federal advisory committee on autism

00:04:58.820 --> 00:05:01.700
held its first meeting after most scientific experts

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:03.700
were replaced with activists and advocates.

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:06.220
The group directed significant attention

00:05:06.220 --> 00:05:08.160
toward the concept of profound autism

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:11.660
which describes individuals needing substantial life-long support

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:14.120
with communication, daily living skills,

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:16.620
and often co-occurring intellectual disability.

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:20.160
This shift marks a notable change in how federal priorities

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:22.340
for autism research and support services

00:05:22.340 --> 00:05:23.960
may be determined going forward.

00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:27.460
You've probably heard recent discussions frame profound autism

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:29.700
as a brand new diagnostic category

00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:32.140
that neatly separates severe cases

00:05:32.140 --> 00:05:33.680
from everyone else on the spectrum.

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:36.260
That framing is misleading because the term

00:05:36.260 --> 00:05:39.360
is not a formal clinical diagnosis in major manuals

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:41.440
like the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:42.760
of Mental Disorders 5.

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:46.060
Instead, it functions as a descriptive concept

00:05:46.060 --> 00:05:49.680
meant to highlight people who require extensive ongoing assistance

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:51.840
and who may have minimal spoken language.

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:55.060
Right now as you listen, roughly one in three

00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:58.580
autistic people meet criteria that would fall under this umbrella

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:00.840
according to recent prevalence estimates.

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:05.320
Yet the boundary between profound and other levels remains fuzzy

00:06:05.320 --> 00:06:07.880
because autism traits exist on overlapping

00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:10.060
continuous rather than clean boxes.

00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:12.720
One memorable number from longitudinal research

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:15.900
shows that about 30% to 40% of autistic children

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:18.340
have co-occurring intellectual disability

00:06:18.340 --> 00:06:20.920
and that figure has remained relatively stable

00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:23.900
even as overall autism identification has risen.

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:27.240
The next time you encounter headlines about autism policy

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:30.020
remember that support needs can change across a lifetime

00:06:30.020 --> 00:06:31.580
and are influenced by environment,

00:06:31.780 --> 00:06:34.920
co-occurring conditions, and access to communication tools.

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:38.280
A practical takeaway is to focus less on the label itself

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:40.560
and more on individualized assessments

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:43.200
that ask what specific communication sensory

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:45.740
and daily living supports a person actually needs.

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:48.640
Watch for research that tracks functional outcomes

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:51.320
over decades instead of cross-sectional snapshots

00:06:51.320 --> 00:06:53.880
because those longitudinal findings tend to reveal

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:56.020
far more about what helps people thrive.

00:06:56.600 --> 00:06:59.480
Before we go keep an eye on how these policy conversations

00:06:59.480 --> 00:07:02.180
around autism and health equity continue to evolve

00:07:02.180 --> 00:07:04.120
as new data emerges next week.

